              "text": "In American football, the jersey numbers worn by linebackers have changed significantly in recent years due to updated rules in the NFL. \n\nToday, linebacker numbers can be split into **traditional ranges** (the historical standard) and **modern ranges** (the result of recent rule changes).\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Traditional Ranges (The Classics)\nFor decades, NFL rules restricted linebackers to very specific number groups. If you see a linebacker wearing these numbers, they are playing in the classic tradition of the position:\n\n*   **50\u201359**: This is the most iconic range for linebackers. Historically, almost all great middle and off-ball linebackers wore a number in the 50s. \n    *   *Famous Examples:* Dick Butkus (#51), Ray Lewis (#52), Brian Urlacher (#54), Junior Seau (#55), Lawrence Taylor (#56), and Jack Lambert (#58).\n*   **90\u201399**: In 1984, the NFL opened up the 90s to linebackers. This range is traditionally worn by **outside linebackers** (especially in 3-4 defensive schemes) who double as pass-rushers/edge defenders.\n    *   *Famous Examples:* T.J. Watt (#90), James Harrison (#92), and DeMarcus Ware (#94).\n\n---\n\n### 2. The Modern NFL Range (0\u201349)\nIn **2021**, the NFL heavily relaxed its jersey number rules, allowing linebackers to wear any number from **1 to 59** (in addition to 90\u201399). In **2023**, the league also allowed players to wear the number **0**. \n\nBecause of this, many modern, fast, and athletic linebackers have ditched the traditional 50s in favor of single digits and teens:\n*   **Single Digits (0\u20139):** Extremely popular for modern, hybrid \"sideline-to-sideline\" linebackers. \n    *   *Examples:* Roquan Smith (#0), Patrick Queen (#6), Quay Walker (#7).\n*   **Teens & Twenties (10\u201329):** Often chosen by linebackers who want to keep the numbers they wore during their college careers.\n    *   *Example:* Micah Parsons (#11).\n\n---\n\n### 3. College Football (NCAA) Rules\nIn college football, jersey numbering rules are much more relaxed than in the NFL. Linebackers are allowed to wear **any number from 0 to 99**. \n*   In college, **single digits (0\u20139)** and **teens (10\u201319)** are highly coveted and usually awarded to the team's best defensive playmakers. \n*   It is also highly common to see college linebackers wearing numbers in the **40s** (e.g., 40, 44, 45), which has a long-standing tradition in college programs but was rarely seen in the modern NFL until recently.\n\n### Summary of What Linebackers Can Wear Today (NFL):\n*   **Allowed numbers:** 0\u201359 and 90\u201399\n*   **Most common \"old-school\" look:** 50\u201359\n*   **Most common \"new-school\" look:** Single digits (0\u20139) or teens",
